              "text": "While there is no physical limit to how many variations of movement you can use to get through water, there are **8 major, widely recognized swimming styles** that a person can learn. \n\nThese are typically divided into **4 competitive strokes** (the Olympic styles) and **4 alternative/survival strokes** (used for recreation, lifesaving, or endurance).\n\n---\n\n### Category 1: The 4 Competitive Strokes\nThese are the standard, highly regulated strokes used in swim meets, school competitions, and the Olympics. \n\n1. **Freestyle (Front Crawl)**\n   * **What it is:** The fastest and most efficient swimming style. Swimmers lie face-down, using alternating arm strokes (a windmill motion) and a continuous flutter kick while turning their head to the side to breathe.\n   * **Best for:** Cardio, weight loss, and general fitness.\n2. **Backstroke**\n   * **What it is:** Essentially a reverse front crawl. You float on your back, alternating your arms in circular, backward motions paired with a flutter kick. \n   * **Best for:** People with back pain, and those who dislike having their face submerged in water (since your nose and mouth remain above water).\n3. **Breaststroke**\n   * **What it is:** One of the oldest and most popular recreational styles, though it is highly technical. It involves sweeping both arms outward in a circle under the water and executing a powerful, frog-like whip kick.\n   * **Best for:** Long-distance swimming, recovery, and beginners.\n4. **Butterfly**\n   * **What it is:** The most physically demanding and complex style. It requires a symmetrical \"dolphin kick\" (both legs moving together) combined with both arms sweeping out of the water simultaneously. \n   * **Best for:** Building intense core and shoulder strength.\n\n---\n\n### Category 2: The 4 Alternative & Survival Strokes\nThese strokes are less about speed and more about efficiency, resting, or specific utility (like lifesaving or military operations).\n\n5. **Sidestroke**\n   * **What it is:** Swimming on your side using a \"scissors kick\" (where the legs open and close like scissors) and an asymmetrical arm-pull. \n   * **Best for:** Lifeguarding (it keeps one arm free to tow a person in distress) and casual, relaxing swims.\n6. **Elementary Backstroke**\n   * **What it is:** A very gentle stroke performed on your back. It uses a modified breaststroke kick and a simple, synchronized arm movement often taught to children using the phrase *\"chicken, airplane, soldier\"* (referring to the hand movements from your armpits, to a T-shape, to your sides).\n   * **Best for:** Survival swimming, resting, or recovering from fatigue in deep water.\n7. **Combat Sidestroke (CSS)**\n   * **What it is:** A highly efficient variation of the sidestroke developed and used by the U.S. Navy SEALs. It combines elements of freestyle, breaststroke, and sidestroke. The swimmer remains largely submerged, making it incredibly stealthy and energy-efficient over massive distances.\n   * **Best for:** Tactical operations, endurance swimming, and energy conservation.\n8. **Trudgen (Trudgen Crawl)**\n   * **What it is:** A historical hybrid stroke. You swim face-down using alternating overarm strokes (like freestyle), but instead of a flutter kick, you perform a scissor kick (like sidestroke) every two strokes. \n   * **Best for:** Open-water swimming, as it allows you to easily lift your head above the water to see where you are going.\n\n---\n\n### Other \"Minor\" Styles You Can Learn\nIf you master all eight of the above, there are still other minor styles, drills, and variations:\n* **Dog Paddle:** The most basic, instinctive way to swim. While not technically efficient, it is the first \"style\" most humans and animals naturally do to keep their heads above water.\n* **Double Overarm Backstroke (Old English Backstroke):** A retro variation where both arms sweep backward simultaneously while on your back.\n* **Artistic / Synchronized Swimming Movements:** Highly specialized aesthetic movements, sculling patterns, and upside-down \"merman\" crawls.\n\n### Where should you start?\nIf you are learning to swim, instructors typically recommend starting with **Freestyle**, **Breaststroke**, and **Elementary Backstroke**. Once those are mastered, you can easily branch out into the remaining styles!",
